        "title": "Aerodynamics: Lift, Drag, and Wings",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Aerodynamics is the application of fluid dynamics to bodies moving through air. Lift and drag are the two fundamental forces. Lift is generated by circulation (Kutta-Joukowski theorem) — a consequence of the pressure difference between upper and lower surfaces. Drag has multiple sources: skin friction (viscous), pressure drag (separation), induced drag (3D wing tip effects), and wave drag (shocks). Wing theory connects complex analysis, boundary layer theory, and the governing equations into a unified framework for designing aircraft."
    }
